Task 1: Monitor CPU Usage
Objective: Check CPU utilization and identify high-usage processes.

Subtask 1.1: Check Overall CPU Usage
Run the following to see real-time CPU statistics:

top
Press q to exit.
Observe the %CPU column to identify high-usage processes.
Subtask 1.2: Get a Snapshot of CPU Usage
Use mpstat (from the sysstat package) for a summary:

sudo dnf install sysstat -y  # Install if not present  
mpstat -P ALL 1 5  # Monitor all cores, 1-second intervals, 5 reports  
Expected Output:

Displays CPU usage per core.
Troubleshooting Tip:

If mpstat is not found, ensure sysstat is installed.
Task 2: Monitor Memory Usage
Objective: Check RAM and swap usage.

Subtask 2.1: Check Free Memory
Use free to see memory and swap usage:

free -h
Expected Output:

Displays total, used, and available memory in human-readable format.
Subtask 2.2: Identify Memory-Hungry Processes
Use top sorted by memory:

top -o %MEM
Press q to exit.
Task 3: Monitor Disk Usage
Objective: Check disk space and I/O activity.

Subtask 3.1: Check Disk Space
Use df for filesystem usage:

df -h
Expected Output:

Shows disk space per mounted filesystem.
Subtask 3.2: Check Directory-Level Disk Usage
Use du to analyze directory sizes:

du -sh /var/log/*  # Check log directory sizes  
Expected Output:

Displays sizes of directories under /var/log/.
Subtask 3.3: Monitor Disk I/O (if iotop is installed)
Install and run iotop to see disk I/O per process:

sudo dnf install iotop -y  
sudo iotop -o  # Show only active I/O processes  
Troubleshooting Tip:

Requires root privileges.
Task 4: Monitor Network Usage
Objective: Check network bandwidth usage.

Subtask 4.1: Check Active Connections
Use ss (modern replacement for netstat):

ss -tulnp  
Expected Output:

Lists active TCP/UDP connections and listening ports.
Subtask 4.2: Monitor Real-Time Network Traffic
Use nload (install if missing):

sudo dnf install nload -y  
nload  
Press q to exit.
Task 5: Analyze Running Processes Impact
Objective: Identify processes consuming excessive resources.

Subtask 5.1: Use ps to Filter High-Resource Processes
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -n 5  # Top 5 CPU-consuming processes  
ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -n 5  # Top 5 memory-consuming processes  
Expected Output:

Lists processes sorted by CPU or memory usage.
Subtask 5.2: Check Container Resource Usage (Podman)
If using Podman, monitor container stats:

podman stats  
Expected Output:

Shows CPU, memory, and network usage per container.
Task 6: Detect Resource Exhaustion
Objective: Identify system bottlenecks.

Subtask 6.1: Check System Logs
journalctl -p err -b  # View error logs since last boot  
Expected Output:

Displays critical system errors.
Subtask 6.2: Simulate High Load (Optional)
Run a stress test (install stress-ng if needed):

sudo dnf install stress-ng -y  
stress-ng --cpu 4 --vm 2 --timeout 30s  
Monitor using top or htop in another terminal.
